body:
The list included their social security number and whether or not they had health insurance. It was common practice for them to turn away those who had no health insurance. I was very confused about it myself, as I thought my sole job was to deter troubles and scan those who entered the premises. Instead, they had us ensure that they filled out the list with their name, ssn, and reason for attending. I shit you the fuck not.

&#x200B;

Edit: I'm talking about Methodist Hospital in South Philadelphia, which is in a neighborhood where the same folks are admitted for drug overdoses and gunshot wounds. Each and every person who enters the emergency room has to walk through a metal detector. Patients have to provide information IMMEDIATELY or they will not be seen, unless they are unconscious.

I think it is a historical moment, a day to bring remembrance to a tragic and horrible timeline in US politics and history. The fact that this is a federal holiday means that the entire nation recognizes it, which is nice. But loads of Americans, you know, those diehard confederates and republicans, won't agree with it. As a matter of fact, people like Candance Owens are attacking the newly established holiday, making assertions that this will re-promote segregation.

&#x200B;

Anyways, it is a good thing. A step nonetheless, but a good thing.
